> Limits, edge cases and honest accounting

The threshold makes acceptance a heuristic relation rather than an equivalence
relation. The artificial spellings AB and AC match; AC and DC match; AB and DC
do not. Thus transitivity cannot be assumed when constructing identity groups.
The retained empty-codex convention considers two empty codexes comparable with
similarity6 and minimum5, yielding match=true even for two nonletter inputs.
Applications needing meaningful names must enforce their own input policy; the
native convention is exposed rather than silently reinterpreted as a valid
identity.

Code preprocessing is O(N+M) time/storage; both codexes are bounded by six
letters, so subsequent cancellation is constant-size. Full kept streams and
actual normalized letters can be long even though final codexes are short.
Case/letter classification follows the active C locale; Unicode normalization
and multilingual pronunciation are outside this byte contract.

Complete codex capture counts original bytes, one actual decision record per
byte and each actual normalized/kept/final-codex character. Pair capture shares
one budget across both codex executions, every actual cancellation record and
every forward residual character. Returned phase copies are not counted again.
Default complete limit is one million; zero disables it. Fields, capacities,
runtime copies and bytes are excluded. Admission precedes each actual
record/string contribution, and failure returns no prefix.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/match_rating_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/match_rating.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<std::pair<std::string, std::string>> pairs{{"AB", "AC"},
                                                           {"AC", "DC"},
                                                           {"AB", "DC"},
                                                           {"123", "---"},
                                                           {"A", "BCDFGH"}};
    std::vector<std::string> labels;
    std::vector<double> scores;
    std::print("| prescribed first input | second input | comparable | actual similarity | actual minimum | " "native heuristic match | actual captured entries |\n| --- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | --- | " "---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < pairs.size(); ++i) {
        auto t =
            dm::algorithms::match_rating_trace(pairs[i].first, pairs[i].second);
        auto &r = t.analysis.result;
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", pairs[i].first,
                   pairs[i].second, r.comparable, r.similarity, r.min_rating,
                   r.match, t.captured_entries);
        if (r.comparable) {
            labels.push_back(i == 3 ? "empty codex"
                             : pairs[i].first + "/" + pairs[i].second);
            scores.push_back(r.similarity);
        }
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(scores, labels, "actual similarity");
    p.abline_h(5, {220, 38, 38}, 1.5).y_limits(0, 7).x_label(
        "prescribed comparable control").y_label("actual native similarity").title("Nontransitivity and the empty-codex convention").save_svg("build/match_rating_controls.svg");
}
```
